            <ArticleTitle>A familial coincidence of pseudotumor cerebri and communicating hydrocephalus.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>727-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A family in which the mother and two of four daughters had a diagnosis of pseudotumor cerebri and one son developed communicating hydrocephalus is described. The other two daughters both have a long history of headaches but no signs of intracranial hypertension. The argument is advanced that there exists a defect of cerebrospinal fluid absorption common to pseudotumor cerebri and communicating hydrocephalus.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
